AD  - Ulusal Manyetik Rezonans Araştirma Merkezi (UMRAM), Ankara, Turkey
AB  - Spatial transcriptomics technologies facilitate the understanding of biological tissue organization by providing gene expression profiles alongside their spatial coordinates. However, defining cellular relationships based solely on physical distance hinders the identification of cells that are biologically similar yet spatially distal. In this study, we present a self-supervised graph neural network model that integrates gene expression, spatial coordinates, and histological images. In the proposed method, tissue topology is modeled using a multimodal hybrid adjacency matrix that incorporates physical distances, gene expression similarity, and image-based morphological similarities. During the learning phase, the model utilizes a contrastive learning strategy to extract consistent features across these different views. This architecture enables the model to identify biologically and morphologically coherent cell clusters within complex tissue regions. © 2026 IEEE.
