AD  - School of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, Northeastern University, Boston, MA, United States
AB  - Tissues affected by disease often exhibit highly heterogeneous genetic compositions. Delineating the spatial distribution of cells, together with their transcriptomic expression profiles, can offer deep insights into disease mechanisms. Accurately mapping cells characterized by single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) onto histological images provides a valuable opportunity to understand diseases at both molecular and morphological levels. However, this task remains challenging due to the high cost and limited resolution of spatial transcriptomics technologies, as well as inconsistencies in gene sets across different platforms. In this study, we present a cross-modal framework, scLocate, that aligns single-cell transcriptomic profiles with histological images using scRNA-seq data. Our approach leverages a pretrained transcriptomics language model (MVTLM), trained via self-supervised learning, and uses spatial transcriptomic data to train a contrastive model. Our method is capable of zeroshot grounding of single cells, even when their gene expression profiles differ from those used during training. We evaluated scLocate using newly proposed benchmarks and metrics, and our results show that scLocate is a novel and practical approach for grounding histological images using scRNA-seq or spatial transcriptomics. Code and related resources are available at github.com/yuanxiaoheben/scLocate. © 2025 IEEE.
